## Migration: `EventProcessor` → .NET 8

### Problems in the Original Code

| API | Issue on .NET 8 |
|-----|-----------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006) |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Obsolete (SYSLIB0046 in .NET 9), unreliable for `WaitOne` wake-up |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | Not cancellation-aware — can't be interrupted cooperatively |

### Patterns Identified

1. **Cancellable work loop** — `while(true)` with no exit condition
2. **Blocking call interruption** — `WaitOne()` that needs to wake on stop
3. **Thread.Abort as fallback kill** — `Stop()` uses Interrupt then Abort

### Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Task? _runTask;

    public void Start()
    {
        _runTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken token)
    {
        try
        {
            await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(token))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Normal shutdown — nothing to do
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        _channel.Writer.TryWrite(item);
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete(); // signal no more items
        _cts.CancelAfter(timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)); // hard stop if drain takes too long

        if (_runTask is not null)
            await _runTask;
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();
        _cts.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

### If You Must Stay Synchronous (no async)

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new();
    private readonly ManualResetEventSlim _newEvent = new(false);
    private Thread? _thread;
    private readonly object _lock = new();

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        var handles = new[] { _newEvent.WaitHandle, _cts.Token.WaitHandle };
        while (!_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Wake on either new item OR cancellation
            WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles);
            _newEvent.Reset();

            while (TryDequeue(out var item))
                Handle(item);
        }
    }

    private bool TryDequeue(out string item)
    {
        lock (_lock)
        {
            if (_queue.Count > 0) { item = _queue.Dequeue(); return true; }
            item = default!; return false;
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel(); // wakes WaitAny via token.WaitHandle
        _thread?.Join(5000);
        // No Abort fallback — thread exits cooperatively
    }

    public void Dispose() => _cts.Dispose();

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Guidance |
|---------|----------|
| **`WaitOne` isn't cancellation-aware** | Use `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle` as one of the handles |
| **No hard-kill exists in .NET 8** | If `Handle()` can hang forever, add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` checkpoints inside it, or move uncooperative work to a child `Process` and use `Process.Kill` |
| **Race between `Set`/`Reset`** | Original code could lose events (multiple `Enqueue` before `Dequeue`). The sync version fixes this with a `while(TryDequeue)` drain loop. The `Channel<T>` version eliminates it entirely |
| **Dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** | It's `IDisposable` — leaking it leaks timers/callbacks |
| **`ManualResetEvent` → `ManualResetEventSlim`** | Lighter weight for intra-process signaling (no kernel object until `WaitHandle` is accessed) |

### Recommendation

Prefer the **Channel-based version** — it eliminates the manual lock, the reset-event race condition, and the thread management entirely. `System.Threading.Channels` is the modern .NET primitive for exactly this producer/consumer pattern.
